  "markdown_report": "## Insights and directions\n\nThe starting evidence suggests three priorities. First, Electronics has 14.95% sampled wrong rankings and a −0.4812 tenth-percentile margin: improving difficult candidate discrimination deserves attention. These training diagnostics do not establish HR@10. Second, users report stale-history influence and weakly supported broad matches; recent behavioral evidence should influence ranking explicitly. Third, embedding similarity scores near 0.54 do not establish severe collapse, so diversity regularization lacks a strong diagnostic basis.\n\nThree literature connections guide feasible directions. [gSASRec](https://www.ijcai.org/proceedings/2024/0939.pdf) motivates learning against multiple negatives, although its calibrated BCE results do not establish sampled-softmax gains here. [LSIDN](https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.14743) motivates separating persistent and transient interests, while warning that both contain noise. [GLSRec](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.datak.2026.102606) motivates directed behavioral transitions; observed transitions cannot establish equipment compatibility or complementarity.\n\n## Framework and candidate ideas\n\n| Direction | Evidence represented | Candidate | Main limitation |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Objective | Candidate discrimination | A: uniform multi-negative softmax | Does not explicitly address stale intent |\n| Temporal routing | Recent versus historical support | B: gated recent-support residual | Recency can overemphasize isolated purchases |\n| Behavioral structure | Directed item transitions | C: sparse transition residual | Sparse edges and popularity confounding |\n\nScores below are prospective judgments, not experimental results. O=originality, P=potential, U=user alignment, M=model alignment; D=difficulty, with larger D meaning harder.\n\n| Idea | O | P | U | M | D |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| A | 3 | 7 | 4 | 9 | 3 |\n| B | 4 | 8 | 8 | 8 | 4 |\n| C | 5 | 7 | 7 | 6 | 5 |\n\nA offers inexpensive discrimination improvements but leaves temporal failures untreated. B directly targets stale evidence and broad, weakly supported matches, but cannot infer practical compatibility. C captures asymmetric behavioral support but cannot label transitions as complements.\n\n## Selected idea: gated recent-support residual\n\nAt 0% progress, choose B. Retain SASRec and add a small scoring branch that pools the last five visible item embeddings using learned exponential decay. A bounded gate combines this recent representation with SASRec’s state. Train the resulting scorer with 32 uniform negatives per position and sampled softmax.\n\nThis explicitly targets two user failures: older isolated interactions dominating current demand, and broad matches outranking candidates supported by recent specific behavior. Multiple competing negatives target the observed lower-tail ranking confusion. These are hypotheses requiring validation; semantic specificity and satisfied durable needs remain unresolved.\n\n```text\nh = SASRec(prefix)\nr = decay_pool(last_five(prefix))\ng = sigmoid(MLP(concat(h, r, h * r)))\nscore(j) = dot(h + g * r, item_embedding[j])\nloss = cross_entropy(scores(positive + 32 negatives), positive_index)\n```\n\nExclude padding and known training positives from negatives. Construct every prefix causally. Compare baseline, objective-only, and full variants using pinned macro validation HR@10 and record GPU cost. Preserve all four routes, seeds, diagnostic interfaces, and identical trained scoring in the predictor writer.",
  "idea": {
    "description": "Add a bounded, learned recent-history residual to SASRec scoring and train with uniform multi-negative sampled softmax. The residual uses the last five visible interactions and learned exponential decay; the gate conditions on the SASRec state, recent representation, and their elementwise interaction.",
    "motivation": "The user diagnosis identifies stale or isolated historical evidence and broad, weakly supported matches as distinct ranking failures. Explicit recent support addresses both as testable hypotheses. Multi-negative training addresses observed candidate confusion, especially Electronics' 14.95% sampled wrong-ranking rate. Neither mechanism establishes compatibility, replacement demand, or actual user acceptance.",
    "implementation_notes": "Keep DATA_DIR = os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'], preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, four independently routed categories, and the SASREC_EPOCHS environment default of 200. Write checkpoints and generated predictor files beneath the trial directory. Use prefix-causal pooling at every supervised position; a candidate gate must never see the next item. Initialize the residual gate conservatively, such as bias -2, to keep initial scores close to SASRec. Sample 32 unique uniform negatives where the eligible catalog permits, excluding padding, the target, and all known training positives; use fewer when necessary. Do not inspect held-out interactions for filtering. Score negatives in chunks if memory requires it and keep execution single-GPU. Preserve DiagnosisProbe(view, device), run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), and diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis). Expose the actual model through view.model and ensure log2feats and predict_score represent the gated scorer used at inference. Preserve existing measured margin definitions; report unavailable category breakdowns as unavailable rather than inventing metadata. Update the predictor writer to reconstruct and load the gate, pooling parameters, and trained embeddings. Compare baseline, objective-only, and full variants within the shared GPU budget; select only by pinned macro validation HR@10. No improvement has yet been measured.",
    "pseudocode": "For each category, retain the baseline training split and routing.\nFor each batch (None, sequence, positive, negative):\n  h = SASRec.log2feats(sequence)\n  r = prefix_causal_decay_pool(item_embeddings(sequence), window=5)\n  g = sigmoid(MLP(concat(h, r, h * r)))\n  features = h + g * r\n  candidates = positive plus up to 32 eligible uniform negatives per valid position\n  logits = dot(features, item_embeddings(candidates))\n  loss = mean(cross_entropy(logits, positive_index=0))\n  update all model parameters\nEvaluate authorized validation candidates using identical scoring.\nGenerate working/predict.py with the same architecture and category routing.\nRun diagnostics using the actual gated scoring model.",
    "originality": {
      "score": 4,
      "positive": "Connects explicit recent behavioral support with multi-candidate discrimination in a small SASRec extension tailored to the supplied diagnoses.",
      "negative": "Gated temporal fusion and multi-negative objectives are established techniques; their combination is incremental."
    },
    "future_potential": {
      "score": 8,
      "positive": "Provides a low-cost foundation for testing whether temporal support explains ranking gains before introducing text encoders or transition graphs.",
      "negative": "Recent interactions can themselves be misleading, and behavioral support cannot resolve unobserved practical requirements."
    },
    "code_difficulty": {
      "score": 4,
      "positive": "Adds a small pooling module and gate while retaining the existing item vocabulary, sequence encoder, and dataset routes.",
      "negative": "Requires careful prefix masking, efficient negative filtering, and synchronized inference and diagnostic scoring."
    }
  },
